Viral Load: \uc0\u8232 \cf4 Current testing technologies are designed to detect viruses at certain levels, but if the viral load is extremely low, it might fall below the detectable threshold. For example, HIV treatments can suppress viral load to levels undetectable by standard tests.\'a0\cf3 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Dormant Viruses: \uc0\u8232 \cf4 Some viruses, like HIV, can go into a latent or dormant state, where they are present in the body but not actively replicating. These viruses may be present in cells, even if the viral load in the blood is undetectable.\'a0\cf3 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Emerging Viral Strains: \uc0\u8232 \cf4 Viruses can mutate and evolve, and new variants may emerge that are resistant to detection by existing tests.\'a0\cf3 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Technological Limitations: \uc0\u8232 \cf4 There are limitations to the sensitivity of current diagnostic tests, and it's possible that some viruses or viral strains may exist at levels that are below the detection limit.\'a0\cf3 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
